🗓️ WEEK 7 — DATABASE AND TWO LOGINS

Date: Monday, 21 September 2026

🎯 Objective

The objective of Week 7 was to start Iteration 2 by fixing the data-loss problem with a database and by adding separate logins for customers and cashiers.

🛠️ Work Completed
Added a SQLite database using Node's built-in node:sqlite module.
Created the tables:
customers
cashiers
products
transactions
cash_shifts
counters
Turned on WAL mode for safe concurrent reads.
Added sign-in for customers with just a 10-digit mobile number.
Added sign-in for cashiers with email and password.
Issued signed login tokens:
Customers: valid for 30 days
Cashiers: valid for 12 hours
Added role checks so each user can only reach their own part of the API.

⚠️ Error / Problem Faced

All bags and bills were lost whenever the server restarted.

After adding the database, every restart inserted the sample products again, so the catalog filled with duplicates.

With one shared login, a customer could open the cashier pages.

Cashier passwords also needed to be stored safely.

✅ Resolution
Stored all permanent data in SQLite, which is a single file and survives restarts.
Used INSERT OR IGNORE on the unique barcode, so existing products are skipped when seeding.
Put the role (customer or cashier) inside every login token and checked it on every request; anyone else gets a 403 error.
Stored cashier passwords as salted scrypt hashes, never as plain text.
Limited wrong login attempts (8 tries per 10 minutes for cashiers).

🖼️ Project Picture

Customer sign-in screen: only a mobile number is needed, with no password or app install.

💡 Learning

We learned that logging in (who you are) and permissions (what you are allowed to do) are two separate problems.

We also learned why passwords must be hashed with a slow, salted algorithm.

